WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.127 --> 00:00:03.878
This isn't a forest.

00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:05.547
Not really.

00:00:05.714 --> 00:00:07.716
It's actually an urban park

00:00:07.716 --> 00:00:11.386
in the densely populated city 
of Nanchang, China.

00:00:11.636 --> 00:00:14.347
Filled with birds, native trees...

00:00:14.723 --> 00:00:19.310
and volcanic rock on 137 acres 
of urban land.

00:00:19.769 --> 00:00:21.229
It's called a sponge city.

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:25.191
And it's a type of natural infrastructure
that architects around the world

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:29.237
are designing to help us combat and 
withstand a changing climate.

00:00:29.779 --> 00:00:32.866
So what does it mean for a city 
to become spongy?

00:00:38.163 --> 00:00:40.623
This video is presented by Delta Airlines.

00:00:42.292 --> 00:00:44.085
Today, like everywhere else on the globe

00:00:44.085 --> 00:00:46.504
cities are feeling the effects 
of climate change.

00:00:46.838 --> 00:00:49.215
And they often don't have 
the right infrastructure

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:51.342
to help them survive its impacts.

00:00:52.177 --> 00:00:54.846
Some neighborhoods in Houston 
don't have enough trees

00:00:54.846 --> 00:00:57.682
to provide shade and relief 
from rising temperatures.

00:00:58.141 --> 00:01:02.687
Meanwhile, rising sea levels threaten
coastal cities, like Mumbai and Seoul

00:01:02.687 --> 00:01:04.689
that don't often have the infrastructure

00:01:04.689 --> 00:01:06.483
to handle more frequent flooding.

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:08.234
This is only made worse

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:10.653
by the fact that cities are covered 
with pavement

00:01:10.653 --> 00:01:13.490
and other surfaces that 
can't absorb water.

00:01:13.656 --> 00:01:15.867
Urbanization changes habitat.

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:18.119
So, when you build on a place

00:01:18.119 --> 00:01:21.915
it removes vegetation and 
alters hydrology.

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:24.125
That's Dr. Charlie Nilon.

00:01:24.375 --> 00:01:28.129
He told me that cities play a complicated role 
in the climate crisis...

00:01:28.171 --> 00:01:31.299
especially when it comes to 
maintaining biodiversity.

00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:33.676
Biodiversity is, simply put

00:01:33.676 --> 00:01:37.514
the total number of plants and animals
that occur someplace.

00:01:39.057 --> 00:01:43.603
Biodiversity is one of our main support systems 
for human life on earth.

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:46.064
We rely on natural ecosystems

00:01:46.064 --> 00:01:49.442
full of diverse plants, animals, 
and organisms

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:53.738
for essential things like clean water, 
food, and medicine.

00:01:54.072 --> 00:01:56.116
And biodiversity is critical

00:01:56.116 --> 00:01:58.660
to defending the Earth 
against climate change.

00:01:59.202 --> 00:02:02.956
We need healthy forests to absorb 
carbon dioxide from the air

00:02:02.956 --> 00:02:06.960
and enough native plants for pollinators
to help crops flourish.

00:02:07.127 --> 00:02:10.004
But studies show that as 
urban expansion continues

00:02:10.004 --> 00:02:11.464
it can drive habitat losses

00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:13.925
that directly put some species in danger...

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:16.636
profoundly impacting global biodiversity.

00:02:16.886 --> 00:02:19.639
Cities have a big impact on
what happens locally.

00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:22.934
Cities capture a significant number 
of plant and animal species...

00:02:23.143 --> 00:02:24.269
which are native.

00:02:24.269 --> 00:02:28.189
So that, even in cities, there's a significant 
amount of biodiversity.

00:02:28.481 --> 00:02:30.650
Which means they can play a role in saving

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:33.153
these local plant and animal species.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:35.697
The really important thing to think about

00:02:35.697 --> 00:02:37.949
is not so much how urbanization...

00:02:38.533 --> 00:02:40.577
reduces biodiversity, but really

00:02:40.577 --> 00:02:42.996
how in cities you can 
conserve biodiversity.

00:02:43.204 --> 00:02:45.748
One way to do this is by 
creating environments

00:02:45.748 --> 00:02:47.292
where nature can flourish.

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:51.629
Environments that can also help cities 
soak up excess water.

00:02:52.005 --> 00:02:54.132
And that's where sponge cities come in.

00:02:55.466 --> 00:02:59.053
In 2015, China launched a 
sponge city pilot program

00:02:59.053 --> 00:03:01.222
in 30 cities across the country.

00:03:01.931 --> 00:03:03.975
The goal of the program was 
to coordinate

00:03:03.975 --> 00:03:06.519
and promote the construction 
of sponge cities...

00:03:06.769 --> 00:03:09.689
which would improve urban drainage 
and flood prevention

00:03:09.689 --> 00:03:12.525
and create a diverse biological environment.

00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:16.905
Architects did this by supplementing 
the existing grey infrastructure

00:03:16.905 --> 00:03:19.449
that relied on concrete pipes and dams

00:03:19.449 --> 00:03:23.536
with natural solutions like gardens 
that are designed to capture rain

00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:26.998
and native trees that suck up excess water
through their roots.

00:03:27.415 --> 00:03:31.336
In China, these ideas were taken 
from ancient drainage systems.

00:03:31.753 --> 00:03:33.254
That's because for centuries

00:03:33.254 --> 00:03:36.049
Chinese cities actually handled water pretty well

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:38.676
in part because they were built 
with nature in mind.

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:41.429
A study from the scientific journal Water

00:03:41.429 --> 00:03:44.015
recreated some of these old practices.

00:03:44.349 --> 00:03:47.185
Building roofs designed with 
sloped eaves allowed rainwater

00:03:47.185 --> 00:03:49.938
to fall onto the permeable pavement below...

00:03:50.146 --> 00:03:53.483
and exterior walls were lined 
with flowers and trees

00:03:53.483 --> 00:03:55.401
to absorb rain and stormwater.

00:03:55.985 --> 00:03:58.446
Today, natural solutions like these have been

00:03:58.446 --> 00:04:01.574
reimplemented in major cities across China.

00:04:04.202 --> 00:04:06.579
In Qian’an, a sewage pipe was replaced

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:08.998
with this natural infiltration system...

00:04:09.123 --> 00:04:12.919
that uses vegetation beds to purify 
storm and rainwater.

00:04:13.795 --> 00:04:15.838
Shanghai replaced concrete roads

00:04:15.838 --> 00:04:18.174
and sidewalks with this permeable pavement

00:04:18.174 --> 00:04:21.177
which is lined with vegetation 
to absorb excess water

00:04:21.177 --> 00:04:22.845
into the soil underground.

00:04:23.471 --> 00:04:26.599
And across Wuhan, dozens of 
urban gardens like these

00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:30.228
were planted to absorb water before 
it overwhelms communities.

00:04:31.437 --> 00:04:34.524
Most of these projects rely on 
introducing vegetation

00:04:34.524 --> 00:04:37.235
in an urban area to soak up excess water.

00:04:37.443 --> 00:04:39.612
This is a really great way to handle flooding

00:04:39.612 --> 00:04:42.573
and it also supports urban biodiversity.

00:04:43.533 --> 00:04:45.910
Because you're providing habitat for additional groups...

00:04:46.244 --> 00:04:47.453
Plant and animal species.

00:04:47.453 --> 00:04:50.206
Sometimes you can restore habitats 
that have been lost.

00:04:50.999 --> 00:04:54.168
Despite these benefits, sponge cities 
can't fix everything.

00:04:54.544 --> 00:04:56.879
The connection between 
climate change and flooding

00:04:56.879 --> 00:04:58.214
is still being studied.

00:04:58.381 --> 00:04:59.882
But when floods do happen

00:04:59.882 --> 00:05:03.386
these spongy cities are only able 
to absorb excess water 

00:05:03.386 --> 00:05:04.929
up to a certain point.

00:05:05.471 --> 00:05:09.183
In 2021, sponge city designs in 
Zhengzhou failed

00:05:09.183 --> 00:05:11.477
when historic rainfall drenched the city.

00:05:11.894 --> 00:05:15.356
More than 300 people died from 
the catastrophic flooding.

00:05:15.815 --> 00:05:17.608
There are other limitations, too.

00:05:17.984 --> 00:05:20.862
One review of sponge cities in 
Guian New District

00:05:20.862 --> 00:05:24.657
found that national standards and codes 
for spongy infrastructure

00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:26.534
are really difficult to enforce

00:05:26.534 --> 00:05:29.996
because climate, hydrology,
and even socio-economics

00:05:29.996 --> 00:05:32.832
are vastly different from one city to the next.

00:05:33.207 --> 00:05:36.127
So what might work in some cities 
won't necessarily

00:05:36.127 --> 00:05:38.796
solve climate-related problems 
in others.

00:05:39.005 --> 00:05:41.194
Plus, ambitious sponge city designs

00:05:41.194 --> 00:05:43.092
like the sprawling parks 
built in China

00:05:43.092 --> 00:05:45.678
are expensive and require a lot of space...

00:05:45.845 --> 00:05:47.430
which most cities don't have.

00:05:47.764 --> 00:05:51.142
So some US cities experimenting 
with natural infrastructure

00:05:51.142 --> 00:05:52.852
like LA  and Boston

00:05:52.852 --> 00:05:55.605
are using the green space 
they already have.

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:59.067
By trying things like planting 
vegetation on curbs...

00:05:59.233 --> 00:06:02.236
and creating green spaces 
along median strips.

00:06:03.071 --> 00:06:04.364
One type of city design

00:06:04.364 --> 00:06:06.783
certainly won't save us 
from climate change...

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:10.328
but they can make a difference 
in how we live with it.

00:06:10.995 --> 00:06:12.914
We often talk about biodiversity

00:06:12.914 --> 00:06:16.125
like it's far away, but it's actually 
right there.

00:06:16.292 --> 00:06:18.044
And I think the more that people
understand that

00:06:18.044 --> 00:06:20.922
the kinds of things they do around
where they live

00:06:20.922 --> 00:06:21.672
that really matters.

00:06:21.756 --> 00:06:24.217
People can do things to change cities.

00:06:24.884 --> 00:06:26.803
They can do things 
to make cities different.

00:06:27.011 --> 00:06:28.846
They can do things 
to make cities better.

